      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Protein kinase C (PKC) isoforms are being elucidated as an increasingly diverse family of enzymes involved in the downstream signal transduction and cell function in various types of cells. To date, 11 PKC isoforms have been identified; they are grouped according to their molecular structure and mode of activation: conventional PKCs (alpha, beta I, beta II, and gamma), novel PKCs (delta, epsilon, mu, theta, and eta), and atypical PKCs (zeta, and iota/lambda). Eosinophils are involved in the pathogenesis of allergic diseases such as bronchial asthma, pollinosis, and atopic dermatitis as well as in the inflammatory response to parasitic infections. Recent studies using selective activators and inhibitors of individual PKC isoforms have revealed that this enzyme is involved in eosinophil dynamics such as cell motility and other functions. However, the role of PKCs in eosinophil functions has been not wholly understood. In this review, we have focused upon and summarized the current knowledge regarding the role of PKC isoforms in eosinophil functions."},
